---
source: crates/dr-workspace/tests/registry.rs
expression: registry().table()
---
asset.get                    query    shared   Read an asset's metadata and live usages in the book (`usages: false` reads the record alone).
asset.list                   query    shared   List assets in the project's built-in asset library (sorted by asset id), each with its folder, its place in it and its live usages (media modules, prose embeds and links, image fields). Set `trashed: true` to include Recently Deleted.
asset.move                   command  book     Put a file of the asset library into another folder (or the top) and/or at another place among that folder's files (manual order).
asset.purge                  command  book     Permanently purge a trashed asset from the book manifest and delete its stored bytes.
asset.put                    command  book     Record an imported file in the project's built-in asset library (bytes live in storage). The same bytes are one file: when the library has them already, that file's id is given back (restored from Recently Deleted if it was there).
asset.read_content           query    shared   Read the content of an asset-library file (extracted text for PDFs with pagination, or metadata and optional bounded data URI for images).
asset.rename                 command  book     Rename a live asset in the built-in asset library.
asset.restore                command  book     Restore a trashed asset from Recently Deleted back into the asset library (to the top when its folder is in Recently Deleted).
asset.trash                  command  book     Move an asset in the built-in asset library to Recently Deleted; places referencing it read as placeholders while its bytes remain.
asset_folder.create          command  book     Make a folder (素材夹) in the asset library, last among the folders of its parent (or the top).
asset_folder.list            query    shared   List the folders of the asset library, in manual order within each parent (with `trashed: true`, those in Recently Deleted too).
asset_folder.move            command  book     Put a folder of the asset library into another folder (or the top) and/or at another place among its siblings; a folder never goes into itself or below itself.
asset_folder.purge           command  book     Permanently purge a folder of the asset library that is in Recently Deleted, with every folder and file in it (their bytes are deleted).
asset_folder.rename          command  book     Rename a folder of the asset library.
asset_folder.restore         command  book     Bring a folder of the asset library back from Recently Deleted with what went there with it (to the top when its parent is still in Recently Deleted).
asset_folder.trash           command  book     Move a folder of the asset library to Recently Deleted with everything in it (its files and folders), in one step: places using those files read as placeholders.
book.get                     query    shared   Read book metadata and project word targets; aggregate counts use the statistics adapter.
book.list                    query    shared   List the library's books (trashed ones when asked); a template's working copy is not one of them.
book.purge                   command  library  Delete a book in Recently Deleted for good, now: its documents, history and assets on this device. Refused for a book not in the trash or still open in an editor. Cannot be undone.
book.purge_expired           command  library  Delete for good every book whose 30 days in Recently Deleted are over (the shelf runs it a while after it opens, so a restore made on another device can arrive first). Cannot be undone.
book.restore                 command  book     Bring a book back from the trash.
book.sequence                query    shared   Read the ordered book sequence: the whole project from the root, book prose only (empty when the project has no book).
book.shelf                   query    shared   Everything the bookshelf shows, in one read: every book (not a template's working copy) with its counts, chapter words, creation, last change, and (in Recently Deleted) when it goes for good. Live books first, most recently changed first; then the trashed ones, most recently deleted first.
book.trash                   command  book     Move a book to the trash (restorable; storage deletes it for good later).
book.update                  command  book     Update selected book metadata fields without replacing other book data.
book.writing_days            query    shared   A book's writing days: words written per day (all devices), today, the last seven days, active days and the streak.
comment.anchors              query    shared   The stored anchors of the comments on one entity's prose, for an editor laying them out (comments without an anchor are not listed).
comment.create               command  book     Add a comment on an entry, any module of an entry, a sub-part inside that module, a passage of prose, or floating.
comment.delete               command  book     Delete a comment. A checklist item's comment is not deleted here: it goes when its line is deleted in the prose.
comment.list                 query    shared   List comments (a target or module sub-part in the trash or gone shows as unlocated).
comment.project              command  book     Keep projected comments from TODO markers (origin = marker) and checklist items (origin = task) in one prose document in step with it: a new marker or checklist item gets a comment; a marker's open comment whose line is gone is deleted and its resolved one stays; a checklist item's comment follows the line's text and tick (ticked = resolved) and is deleted with the line, resolved or not; deterministic ids merge across devices.
comment.reanchor             command  book     Put a comment's anchor back where the editor's undo brought its text back (the author's change, synced; it follows the editor's undo, so it is not an entry of the object-level undo).
comment.restore_body         command  book     Undo or redo a change of a comment's text: bring back `body` while the text still reads `expected`; any other change since refuses it. Status, resolution time and linked conversation are not touched.
comment.update               command  book     Change a comment: its text, status (resolve / reopen; a checklist item's comment ticks or clears the item in the prose) or linked Agent conversation. A checklist item's comment takes no other text: its words are the line's, so change the line in the prose.
custom_module.adopt          command  library  Keep a module that came with someone else's template or book in my modules (new id); this project's entry templates use it from now on and follow it.
custom_module.create         command  library  Make a new module in my modules from a module value (format drifting.module); it gets a new id.
custom_module.export         query    shared   Write one of my modules to a .driftingmodule file (the same module is the same bytes every time).
custom_module.get            query    shared   Read one of my modules and its value.
custom_module.import         command  library  Validate a .driftingmodule file and add it to my modules as a new module (always a new one, with a new id).
custom_module.list           query    shared   List my modules (custom entry modules kept in this library), most recently changed first.
custom_module.remove         command  library  Delete one of my modules for good. Projects using it keep their copies and stop following it.
custom_module.rename         command  library  Rename one of my modules; every project using it gets the new name.
custom_module.save_as        command  library  Save a module this project uses as a new module of mine (new id) and make this project's entry templates use it; the module it used before is not changed.
custom_module.sync_copies    command  book     Bring this project's copies of my modules up to date (a project does this itself when it is opened and when a module of mine changes); entries keep their values.
custom_module.update         command  library  Replace one of my modules with a new value, whole; every project using it gets the new one (entries keep their values).
custom_module.uses           query    shared   The projects (not in Recently Deleted, not a template's working copy) whose entry templates use one of my modules.
entry.create                 command  book     Create an entry either residing under `parent` (without membership) or attached to `host` (residing in `host`'s home, `host` itself when `level`, or the root).
entry.get                    query    shared   Read an entry including retained module values; an entry whose switch is on also carries what lies below it (resolved settings and their sources, children, members).
entry.list                   query    shared   Find entries, or list the entries living under one in manuscript order; with below, each listed entry whose switch is on carries its resolved settings, children and members.
entry.mentions               query    shared   List the paragraphs of the book's prose that link to an entry (its name or an alias linked automatically, counted while the entry's own automatic linking and the author's setting are on, or a link the author picked with @, always counted), in manuscript order, each with the entry and module it is in, its document, paragraph number and block ID, how many links to the entry it holds and the text around the entry's name. `total` counts the paragraphs, `links` the links. At most `limit` paragraphs (default 20, at most 50) from `from`; `next` is the `from` of the next page. Paragraphs in entries in Recently Deleted only with `trashed: true`. Text that names the entry without a link is found with entry.search.
entry.move                   command  book     Move an entry's residence and its position in the folder's manual order (`index` among the folder's stored children). Moving where an entry lives never hangs it on a lane or moves it on a timeline scale.
entry.purge                  command  book     Delete an entry in Recently Deleted for good, with the entries that went in with it: their bodies, checkpoints, memberships, placements, relation memberships and canvas coordinates. An entry below it that went in on its own moves up to where it lived. Refused for an entry not in Recently Deleted. Cannot be undone.
entry.restore                command  book     Restore an entry and what lives under it from trash; if its stored residence can no longer hold entries, relocate it to the nearest live level ancestor or the root.
entry.search                 query    shared   Search the book's entries for a word or phrase, matched as written (case-insensitive, not split into words): titles, field values, key/value rows, patches, plot cells and prose, not templates or comments. Hits come in manuscript order, each with its entry, its module and the text around the match; a prose hit (one per paragraph) also gives its document, paragraph number and block ID. At most `limit` hits (default 20, at most 50) from `from`; `next` is the `from` of the next page. Entries in Recently Deleted only with `trashed: true`. A hit is not a read of the prose.
entry.set_auto_link          command  book     Turn an entry's own automatic linking (「自动链接」) on or off: whether its title and aliases link by themselves in the book's text, and whether the automatic links already pointing at it show as links. Turned off, those links read as plain text and their data stays; turned on, they show again and its names already written get linked in the background. Links picked with @ show either way. Refused when, turned on, one of its names is already another linked entry's.
entry.set_below_auto_link    command  book     Turn a level's automatic linking (「自动链接」) on or off: entries made to live in it from now on start with their own switch that way, and every entry living below it now (however deep, not those in Recently Deleted, not containers whose template does not follow its level) gets its own switch set the same, in one step one undo takes back; turned on, their names already written get linked in the background. Each entry can be changed on its own afterwards. Refused when a name would then belong to two linked entries.
entry.set_level              command  book     Turn an entry's level switch on or off. Turning it off moves all live descendants below it to the trash in place (leaving already-trashed entries, overrides, memberships and swimlanes untouched) in one commit that one undo reverts.
entry.trash                  command  book     Trash an entry with what lives under it, keeping entries that only belong to it.
entry.update                 command  book     Rename an entry, set its mark, switch its template or change the overrides of what lives below it, without deleting retained content or touching its switch.
entry_history.restore        command  book     Restore selected entry metadata (switch included) or structural records from a snapshot as a new undoable commit.
entry_history.version        query    shared   Read the current book version for per-entry snapshot restoration.
entry_snapshot.capture       command  book     Capture changed entry content periodically or on close using the existing history interval; unchanged content is skipped.
entry_snapshot.create        command  book     Save a complete entry checkpoint, including retained modules and all body versions. Call before an Agent writes the entry.
entry_snapshot.create_with_receipt command  book     Save a complete entry checkpoint and return its exact committed versions for a guarded host write. Never infer these versions from a later observation.
entry_snapshot.list          query    shared   List complete checkpoints of one entry, newest first.
entry_snapshot.preview       query    shared   Read all historical entry content and current version guards for an atomic restore.
entry_snapshot.restore       command  book     Atomically restore all owned content of one entry with required version guards, preserving a complete pre-restore checkpoint.
entry_template.delete        command  book     Delete an unused template without deleting its retained prose documents.
entry_template.list          query    shared   List the templates of a book: modules, the switch's default, linkable, settings and actions.
entry_template.put           command  book     Create or replace a template definition (any modules, the switch's default, linkable, settings, actions) while retaining existing entry content and switches.
entry_template.put_module    command  book     Add or update a module definition of any kind on a template.
entry_template.remove_module command  book     Remove a module definition while retaining values and prose on existing entries.
fact.list                    query    shared   List the book's project facts (项目设定), in order.
fact.put                     command  book     Add a project fact or change one (key, value, position).
fact.remove                  command  book     Remove a project fact.
history.points               query    shared   List a prose document's history points (kept 30 days, at most one every 15 minutes of writing), newest first, with the words at each.
history.preview              query    shared   Read a prose document as it was at a history point: its paragraphs (with H1/H2/H3 levels) and its owner's title and summary then.
history.restore_point        command  book     Restore one template-module prose document from history; entry bodies require complete-entry snapshots.
layout.clear                 command  book     Clear entry coordinates (or the whole `<scope>/<mode>` view) in `layout:<book>` (not undoable; leaves the book document untouched).
layout.get                   query    shared   Read the free-canvas coordinates stored for a `<scope>/<mode>` view in `layout:<book>`.
layout.put                   command  book     Set entry coordinates in a `<scope>/<mode>` view of `layout:<book>` (not undoable; leaves the book document untouched).
membership.add               command  book     Add or order a membership without moving its residence.
membership.list              query    shared   List valid memberships in a book.
membership.primary           command  book     Choose an existing membership as primary, or clear the primary membership.
membership.remove            command  book     Remove a membership and any timeline placements left without a lane.
membership.restore           command  book     Restore the selected effects of a membership cancellation without replacing entries or timeline views.
memory.add                   command  book     Add a Project Memory entry (plain text; takes effect at once).
memory.list                  query    shared   List the book's Project Memory entries.
memory.remove                command  book     Remove a Project Memory entry (kept as removed; restorable).
memory.restore               command  book     Bring back a removed Project Memory entry.
memory.update                command  book     Change a Project Memory entry's text, kind or status.
module.add_custom_item       command  book     Add an item to a list in an entry's custom module (at `index`, or last); returns the new item's id.
module.edit_kv               command  book     Change the key or the value of one key/value row of a module, leaving what is not named as it is.
module.edit_patch            command  book     Change the title or the body of one patch record, leaving what is not named as it is.
module.list                  query    shared   List an entry's current and retained modules and their registered prose documents.
module.move_custom_item      command  book     Move an item of a list in an entry's custom module to another place; its values move with it.
module.prose_document        command  book     Initialize an entry's or a template's prose module and return its document ID.
module.put_grid              command  book     Replace the rows, columns, cells and size of one plot module.
module.put_kv                command  book     Add or update an ordered key/value row in a module.
module.put_media             command  book     Add or update an ordered file item in an entry's media module.
module.put_patch             command  book     Create or edit the text of a patch module record, preserving its source reference.
module.read                  query    shared   Read a current or retained module and its structured content; a children module reads what lies below the entry.
module.read_custom           query    shared   Read an entry's custom module: the book's copy of the module (or that it is broken), and the values the copy can read, by block id.
module.read_prose            query    shared   Read an entry's or a template's prose module with block IDs and version.
module.remove_custom_item    command  book     Remove an item, with what it keeps, from a list in an entry's custom module.
module.remove_kv             command  book     Remove a key/value row from a module.
module.remove_media          command  book     Remove a file item from an entry's media module.
module.remove_patch          command  book     Remove one patch record from a patches module; the entry and its other records stay.
module.replace_prose         command  book     Replace an exact passage in an entry's prose module, addressed by block ID.
module.set_arrangement       command  book     Choose how this entry lays out the files of its media module (`featured`, `grid` or `list`), or go back to its template's (`null`); the template is not changed.
module.set_custom_value      command  book     Set or clear one block's value in an entry's custom module, checked against the block's kind; a block in a list is reached through its items (`path`).
module.set_field             command  book     Set or clear a typed field value in an entry's fields module.
module.set_prose             command  book     Write paragraphs of an entry's or a template's prose module: all of them, in order, as module.read_prose reads them. Unchanged paragraphs stay; one whose words change is edited in place and keeps its id and comments. A list item has `list` (bullet, ordered or task) and `list_level`. A task item is a checklist item and also a comment whose words are the line's: `checked` ticked resolves it, cleared reopens it, and taking the line out deletes the comment.
project.create               command  library  Create a project from a project template (defaults to the built-in writing template).
project.get                  query    shared   Read the project definition: template source, Agent instructions, book switch and word targets, the left-sidebar tabs (「全部」 always among them), the tab the project is on, and the template it is the working copy of.
project.update               command  book     Update the project definition: Agent instructions, whether the project has a book, and word targets; with the project's name and profile, all in one undoable write.
project_template.edit        command  library  Edit a saved project template in its working copy: a project made from it the way project.create makes one, kept on this device and out of the project lists, recording no writing days; every change made in it saves itself over the template. A working copy left from before is used again when it is in step with the template.
project_template.export      query    shared   Export a saved project template to a deterministic .driftingtemplate file.
project_template.get         query    shared   Read a saved project template and its validated value.
project_template.import      command  library  Validate and import a .driftingtemplate file as a new saved project template in this library.
project_template.list        query    shared   List saved project templates in this library.
project_template.remove      command  library  Permanently delete a saved project template from this library.
project_template.save        command  library  Save a project's structure and selected sample items as a project template, or a template's working copy over that template.
project_template.update      command  library  Rename a saved project template or update its description.
project_template.working_copies query    shared   List the templates' working copies still on this device, oldest first.
prose.link                   command  book     Link anchored ranges of prose documents to entities (anchors, not positions). Links to entries count as the author's own: shown even where automatic linking is off.
prose.read                   query    shared   Read paragraphs of a prose document (text, block id, heading level; a list item's kind and level, a checklist item's tick).
prose.replace                command  book     Replace one exact passage of prose with new text (it must occur exactly once, in one paragraph).
prose.restore                command  book     Restore a prose document to an earlier version, as a new change (history stays).
prose.restore_links          command  book     Undo or redo a change of entity links on anchored spans of prose documents: on each span put back `value` while the link there still reads `expected` (a span already reading `value` is left alone). A link changed otherwise since refuses the whole call before anything is written.
prose.shape                  query    shared   Shape of a prose document: paragraphs, sentences, the share of dialogue, its checklist, the files it embeds and the files it links to (with those in Recently Deleted).
prose.unlink                 command  book     Take links to entities off anchored ranges of prose documents (anchors, not positions).
recent.list                  query    shared   List this device's live pages, retaining each entry's selected module.
recent.touch                 command  book     Record an entry and optional module on this device without an undo step.
relation_row.add_entry       command  book     Add a live entry to one side of a row. Every new pair must be unique within the relation.
relation_row.create_group    command  book     Create one exact relation row from two nonempty disjoint live-entry sets, in one undo step. Refuse stale direction or any pair already in another row.
relation_row.create_pair     command  book     Relate two distinct live entries. Reuse a matching row or start a new one. Return the row ID, with no write if the pair already exists.
relation_row.delete          command  book     Delete a row. Removing the last row also removes the relation.
relation_row.remove_entry    command  book     Remove an entry from a row. An empty side removes the row; removing the last row also removes the relation.
relation_type.create         command  book     Create a named relation with a description and direction, initially without rows.
relation_type.delete         command  book     Delete a relation and all its rows in one edit.
relation_type.list           query    shared   List each relation with its description, direction and rows of live entries. Each row relates every left entry to every right entry.
relation_type.rename         command  book     Rename a relation. Normalized names are unique within the project.
relation_type.restore        command  book     Undo or redo one relation write: put back or take out the pairs, definition values and relations it names while they still read as that write left them. Anything changed since refuses the whole step; what others added stays.
relation_type.set_description command  book     Edit a relation's description, which can contain links to entries.
relation_type.set_direction  command  book     Change the direction of every row of a relation, preserving its left and right entries.
tab.move                     command  book     Change a left-sidebar tab's position (「全部」 included).
tab.put                      command  book     Add a left-sidebar tab that mounts a level entry or the book sequence; one thing is mounted on one tab. A tab cannot mount a view (views go with the bottom panel).
tab.remove                   command  book     Remove a left-sidebar tab (not what it mounts); 「全部」 stays.
tab.select                   command  book     Remember the left-sidebar tab the project is on, for every device (kept in `layout:<book>`; not undoable; leaves the book document untouched).
timeline_view.add_lane       command  book     Add one live lane to a view without replacing existing lane records or changing memberships. A lane is an entry that takes entries from elsewhere (「收别处的条目」 on); an entry with only its level switch on is refused. The lane holds what is hung on it, not what lives under it.
timeline_view.delete         command  book     Delete a timeline view while retaining lane entries and memberships; lanes leaving their last live view save the scale order of what is hung on them back to the memberships' manual order in the same commit.
timeline_view.list           query    shared   List timeline views with their effective lanes, placements and time pins.
timeline_view.move_lane      command  book     Change only one stored lane's order in a view, preserving hidden data and other lane positions.
timeline_view.place          command  book     Place or move an entry in a timeline, optionally joining a lane in the same commit.
timeline_view.put            command  book     Create or edit a timeline view, including its independent lane order.
timeline_view.put_pin        command  book     Add or edit a time pin, optionally bound to any live entry.
timeline_view.remove         command  book     Remove an entry from a timeline lane and prune positions where it has no remaining lane.
timeline_view.remove_lane    command  book     Remove one view lane and newly orphaned visible positions, saving the scale order of what is hung on it back to the memberships' manual order when leaving its last live view.
timeline_view.remove_pin     command  book     Remove a time pin from a timeline view.
timeline_view.rename         command  book     Rename a timeline view without changing its lanes, positions or pins.
timeline_view.restore_fields command  book     Restore selected stored timeline fields for undo or redo without replacing the rest of a view.
timeline_view.set_lane_act_track command  book     Switch whether a stored lane in a view is displayed as a top act track.
writing_day.record           command  book     Set this device's words for one day of a book (the writing-day recorder; not undoable).
